PATCH: zephyr_shell_telnet_replace_stale_client — let a new telnet shell session replace the old one
=================================================================================================

Written against: Zephyr 4.5.0-dev  (shell_telnet.c as shipped in /opt/toolchains/zephyr on 2026-06-29)
Target platform: any   (shell backend behavior only)
Application:     /workspace/projects/hello_eink

SUMMARY
-------
Zephyr's built-in telnet shell backend is single-client. When a second client
connects while one session is already active, `telnet_accept()` currently logs
`Telnet client already connected.` and drops the new socket.

For this project, that policy is awkward in practice: stale telnet sessions are
common during native_sim bring-up and remote debugging, and the operator
usually wants the *new* connection to take over immediately.

WHAT THE PATCH DOES
-------------------
Changes `subsys/shell/backends/shell_telnet.c` so that when a new telnet client
arrives and one is already connected, the backend cleanly closes the old client
connection first and then binds the shell to the new socket.

Behavior after this patch:

  - first telnet client connects normally
  - second telnet client connects and takes ownership of the shell
  - the older client gets disconnected

RATIONALE
---------
This keeps the backend single-client, which matches Zephyr shell's current
design, but flips the admission policy to the operator-friendly direction.
This is lower risk than attempting multi-client shell fanout.

---

--- a/subsys/shell/backends/shell_telnet.c
+++ b/subsys/shell/backends/shell_telnet.c
@@ -363,10 +363,12 @@ static void telnet_accept(struct zsock_pollfd *pollfd)
 		return;
 	}
 
-	if (sh_telnet->fds[SOCK_ID_CLIENT].fd > 0) {
-		/* Too many connections. */
-		ret = 0;
-		LOG_ERR("Telnet client already connected.");
-		goto error;
+	if (sh_telnet->fds[SOCK_ID_CLIENT].fd >= 0) {
+		LOG_INF("Replacing existing Telnet client with newer connection");
+		telnet_end_client_connection();
+		if (sh_telnet->fds[SOCK_ID_CLIENT].fd >= 0) {
+			ret = -EBUSY;
+			goto error;
+		}
 	}
 
 	sh_telnet->fds[SOCK_ID_CLIENT].fd = sock;